Run:  python3 book_demo.py              (prints the whole book)
      python3 book_demo.py 3            (prints chapter 3 only)
      python3 book_demo.py search WORD  (finds WORD across chapters)
      python3 book_demo.py html         (writes book.html)
"""
import json, sys, html as _h

BOOK = {'title': "The Dead Man's Cay Tide", 'author': 'Signature original', 'genre': 'Ghost Ships', 'chapters': [{'n': 1, 'title': 'The Chart', 'text': "That night Captain Maria Santos stood at the wheel with a royal pardon's copy memorized and the original safe. Stars overhead, the privateer astern, Dead Man's Cay behind. the sea tests all — the sea was about to test it.\n\nthe privateer's sail was spotted on the third day — a black speck that didn't go away. 'They want a royal pardon,' the ship's surgeon said. Captain Maria Santos nodded. 'Then they'll have to take it.' The crew cleared for action, grinning. They'd signed for this.\n\nThe crew signed on with the articles read aloud: shares, rules, no desertion. Captain Maria Santos added one article of their own: 'the sea tests all.' The crew cheered. Pirates are sentimentalists at heart."}, {'n': 2, 'title': 'The Chase', 'text': "They took the privateer's survivors aboard in irons — pirates have rules about the sea, even for enemies. 'The island eats ships,' Captain Maria Santos told the prisoners. 'Let's see if it eats villains too.'\n\nthe privateer closed in at the strait — the only passage, and the privateer across it like a door. The parley was short: 'a royal pardon for your lives.' Captain Maria Santos's answer was shorter, and involved cannon.\n\nThe fight was ship to ship, rail to rail, cutlass to cutlass. the ship's surgeon led the boarders with a roar. Captain Maria Santos dueled the privateer's captain on the quarterdeck while the tide burned around them. When it was done, the privateer's ship was sinking and theirs was afloat — barely."}, {'n': 3, 'title': "Dead Man's Cay", 'text': "Inland: jungle, then ruins, then the cave. a royal pardon's X marked a chamber sealed a century. Inside: not gold — at first. A tomb. A logbook. The real a royal pardon: the captain's confession, naming every cache along Dead Man's Cay.\n\nThat's when the privateer arrived — escaped, vengeful, with the prisoners turned crew. The standoff in the cave, by torchlight, with a royal pardon between them. 'Share?' the privateer suggested. Captain Maria Santos laughed. Pirates share. Up to a point.\n\nThe gold was there too — don't worry, there was gold — but Captain Maria Santos understood: a royal pardon was bigger than one chest. It was a lifetime of plunder, mapped. Enough to buy a fleet. Or a pardon. Or a future."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Reckoning', 'text': "Off the headland, Captain Maria Santos opened the logbook one last time. So many caches. So many voyages. the sea tests all — the sea had tested it, and it held.\n\nThey sailed for Dead Man's Cay with the gold and a royal pardon and the logbook's secrets. The crew's shares would make them rich. Captain Maria Santos's share would make them legend. the ship's surgeon was already spending theirs on a tavern.\n\nThey made the ship with the tide and the devils both rising. the privateer made it too — of course; villains are buoyant. The final fight was on the beach, in the surf, with a royal pardon in Captain Maria Santos's coat and everything to lose."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'Port and Pardon', 'text': "a royal pardon hangs in the tavern now, framed, with the articles beneath it. Tourists gawk. Old sailors nod. the sea tests all — carved, at the ship's surgeon's insistence, on the frame.\n\nSome of the crew shipped out again, chasing the logbook's caches. Captain Maria Santos stayed — for a season. The sea calls, and Captain Maria Santos always answers. But home, Captain Maria Santos was learning, is also an answer.\n\nCaptain Maria Santos took the logbook to the governor — not the privateer's governor, the new one — and traded certain pages for a pardon, signed and sealed. The crew cheered. Respectability, it turns out, is its own adventure."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'The Tide Returns', 'text': "The new crew was young and wide-eyed. Captain Maria Santos read them the articles, including the added one. They cheered, the way crews do. Pirates are sentimentalists. It's why they survive.\n\nDead Man's Cay shrank astern again, beloved and boring in the best way. Ahead: a royal pardon, the sea, the test. Captain Maria Santos stood at the wheel and felt the old lift. This is the life.\n\nA year later, a new a royal pardon surfaced — they always do — and Captain Maria Santos was packed before the ink dried. the ship's surgeon closed the tavern for 'renovations' and came along, because that's what friends do."}]}

def word_count():
    return sum(len(c["text"].split()) for c in BOOK["chapters"])

def print_book(ch=None):
    print("=" * 64)
    print(BOOK["title"].upper(), "\nby", BOOK["author"], "-", BOOK["genre"])
    print("=" * 64)
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        if ch and c["n"] != ch:
            continue
        print("\n\nCHAPTER %d: %s\n%s" % (c["n"], c["title"], "-" * 40))
        print(c["text"])
    print("\n[%d words, %d chapters — complete]" % (word_count(), len(BOOK["chapters"])))

def search(word):
    w = word.lower()
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        for i, para in enumerate(c["text"].split("\n\n")):
            if w in para.lower():
                print("[ch %d, para %d] %s..." % (c["n"], i + 1, para[:160].replace("\n", " ")))

def write_html(path="book.html"):
    parts = ["<html><head><meta charset='utf-8'><title>%s</title></head><body>" % _h.escape(BOOK["title"])]
    parts.append("<h1>%s</h1><p><i>by %s — %s</i></p>" % (_h.escape(BOOK["title"]), _h.escape(BOOK["author"]), _h.escape(BOOK["genre"])))
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        parts.append("<h2>Chapter %d: %s</h2>" % (c["n"], _h.escape(c["title"])))
        for para in c["text"].split("\n\n"):
            parts.append("<p>%s</p>" % _h.escape(para))
    parts.append("</body></html>")
    open(path, "w").write("\n".join(parts))
    print("wrote", path)

if __name__ == "__main__":
    a = sys.argv[1:]
    if a and a[0] == "html":
        write_html()
    elif len(a) >= 2 and a[0] == "search":
        search(a[1])
    elif a and a[0].isdigit():
        print_book(int(a[0]))
    else:
        print_book()

